WEBVTT

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:04.859
This is the Setu walkthrough. Morgan is the
developer on Relay desktop.

00:00:04.859 --> 00:00:09.488
Casey hosts a Pixel on Field for Android.
These two prototypes replay

00:00:09.488 --> 00:00:12.960
the same story with sample data and
simulated connections.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:17.876
Start with Create account on each app and
enter an email address.

00:00:17.876 --> 00:00:21.584
Returning users choose Sign in and follow
the same verification flow.

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:26.670
Enter the six digit email code. If a code
expires or does

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:30.911
not match, edit the address or request a
new code after the

00:00:30.911 --> 00:00:34.444
cooldown. This code signs you in. Device
pairing comes later.

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:39.999
Both apps offer Solo and Corporate. For
this personal session, choose Solo.

00:00:39.999 --> 00:00:44.667
Corporate will use organization enrollment
and a device grant, which we will

00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:45.834
walk through later.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:51.330
Field gives Casey one next step. Open All
setup checks to see

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:55.930
Wi-Fi, Developer options, and Wireless
debugging. USB debugging is available for
wired

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:58.229
workflows and can stay off here.

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:03.207
Open About phone and tap Build number seven
times. On some phones,

00:01:03.207 --> 00:01:07.383
it is under Software information. The guide
shows what to search for

00:01:07.383 --> 00:01:09.819
if your Settings menu is arranged
differently.

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:14.626
After the seventh tap, return to Setu and
run the readiness check

00:01:14.626 --> 00:01:18.622
again. Android may ask you to confirm the
phone screen lock during

00:01:18.622 --> 00:01:18.954
setup.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:24.776
On a supported Android phone, join Wi-Fi,
go to Developer options, and

00:01:24.776 --> 00:01:29.722
turn on Wireless debugging. Confirm the
network if Android asks. Android debugger

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:33.020
authorization is a separate prompt from
Setu pairing.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:38.295
The required checks passed. Casey taps
Start hosting and reads the Setu

00:01:38.295 --> 00:01:42.761
pairing code to Morgan. It expires, and
Casey can renew it. The

00:01:42.761 --> 00:01:46.110
phone is now ready to receive an access
request.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:50.655
In Relay, choose Connect device and enter
the code from the phone.

00:01:50.655 --> 00:01:54.380
An expired code keeps you in the form and
explains how to

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.484
recover. Ask Casey to renew or read the
current code.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:02.549
Morgan submits the current code and sees
Awaiting approval. Casey checks the

00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:06.768
requester and the requested access on
Android. They can decline the request

00:02:06.768 --> 00:02:08.174
and still keep hosting.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:13.395
After Casey approves, Setu opens an
encrypted tunnel and gives Morgan a

00:02:13.395 --> 00:02:17.760
local endpoint. The ready state means the
tunnel is listening. The debugger

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.214
still needs to attach.

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:24.421
Run the copied ADB connect command on the
computer. Then select the

00:02:24.421 --> 00:02:28.812
phone in Android Studio. If the command is
unavailable, check Platform Tools.

00:02:28.812 --> 00:02:33.204
If Android asks you to trust the debugger,
approve it on the

00:02:33.204 --> 00:02:33.569
phone.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:38.169
Both screens now show ADB attached. Morgan
can run or debug the

00:02:38.169 --> 00:02:41.908
app in the IDE, and Casey can still see who
is connected

00:02:41.908 --> 00:02:43.154
and end the session.

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:48.334
Move Relay to the tray to continue working
in the IDE. If

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:52.638
Casey leaves Field, Android shows an
ongoing notification from the foreground
service.

00:02:52.638 --> 00:02:56.224
Either person can end the session from the
background controls.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:02.518
On a network interruption, Setu shows
Reconnecting. If automatic recovery
expires, check

00:03:02.518 --> 00:03:08.005
Wi-Fi and hosting, then retry. An
unavailable device stays visibly offline
until

00:03:08.005 --> 00:03:09.834
the connection is restored.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:15.009
Open Evidence and choose Take screenshot or
Record screen. Android asks whether

00:03:15.009 --> 00:03:19.288
to share one app or the entire screen each
time capture begins.

00:03:19.288 --> 00:03:23.567
If Casey declines or Android hides
protected content, choose a permitted
capture

00:03:23.567 --> 00:03:25.349
or import an existing file.

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:29.888
Review the image and write a short note
with the action and

00:03:29.888 --> 00:03:33.546
result. A useful report says what was
tapped, what happened, and where

00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:36.289
to look. Keep sensitive content out of the
capture.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:41.292
Review the file name and note one more
time, then choose the

00:03:41.292 --> 00:03:45.454
developer inbox or an allowed Android share
destination. If sharing fails, the

00:03:45.454 --> 00:03:47.534
local draft stays available for retry.

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:53.066
Record screen follows the same consent
flow. The recording indicator and Stop

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:57.702
recording control stay available. Stop the
clip, review what it contains, and

00:03:57.702 --> 00:04:00.019
share it with the same context.

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:05.346
Use Import media when the screenshot or
clip already exists. Android’s picker

00:04:05.346 --> 00:04:09.863
opens first. The imported item follows the
same preview, note, and sharing

00:04:09.863 --> 00:04:11.744
flow as a fresh capture.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:17.146
On desktop, Evidence can import a
screenshot or recording from the local

00:04:17.146 --> 00:04:21.662
machine. Open the preview, add the
reproduction steps, and keep a draft

00:04:21.662 --> 00:04:25.049
or continue to share. Export saves a local
copy.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:30.376
Choose the review folder and confirm
sharing. This walkthrough uses simulated
destinations.

00:04:30.376 --> 00:04:34.822
In the product, this is where an authorized
upload would make the

00:04:34.822 --> 00:04:36.674
evidence available to the reviewer.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:41.480
The confirmation tells Morgan the evidence
was shared. Casey can also see

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:45.430
the status of their own evidence. The
evidence file and note stay

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:47.405
separate from the session audit log.

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:52.306
Morgan opens Disconnect in the current
session. Casey can also choose End

00:04:52.306 --> 00:04:56.382
session on the phone or in the
notification. Ending the session closes

00:04:56.382 --> 00:04:59.100
the tunnel and releases the local ADB
endpoint.

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:04.324
History records the device and how the
session ended. Casey can stop

00:05:04.324 --> 00:05:08.717
hosting separately so the phone stops
accepting new requests. On desktop, Forget

00:05:08.717 --> 00:05:12.744
device removes a saved pairing when reuse
is no longer wanted.

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:18.013
On a managed account or phone, Solo can be
locked by the

00:05:18.013 --> 00:05:22.397
organization. Both screens explain the
decision. The real product must enforce it

00:05:22.397 --> 00:05:25.684
in the session service, Android host, and
organization gateway.

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:31.124
Corporate access requires verified
membership, device enrollment, and a device
grant. The

00:05:31.124 --> 00:05:35.719
phone owner can ask an administrator for
help when enrollment is pending.

00:05:35.719 --> 00:05:39.164
An email address by itself grants no device
access.

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:44.524
In Corporate on Relay, choose Select device
and pick an enrolled phone

00:05:44.524 --> 00:05:49.019
you’ve been granted. Restricted or offline
devices show a reason. In a

00:05:49.019 --> 00:05:52.390
managed deployment, these sessions travel
through the organization relay.

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:57.903
Authorized users open Audit log and export
the report. The report records

00:05:57.903 --> 00:06:02.577
session events and may include operation
details when available. Screenshots and
recordings

00:06:02.577 --> 00:06:04.134
are shared from Evidence.

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:09.878
If the account lacks membership or its
device grant, ask the administrator.

00:06:09.878 --> 00:06:14.726
If Android debugging is blocked by device
policy, hosting stays unavailable. A

00:06:14.726 --> 00:06:17.150
revoked grant ends the existing session.

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:23.036
When a destination is blocked by corporate
policy, the draft stays available.

00:06:23.036 --> 00:06:28.043
Casey can select the approved organization
inbox. Personal and managed evidence live

00:06:28.043 --> 00:06:29.294
in separate workspaces.

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:35.710
Profile and settings show email and the
current workspace. Relay exposes the

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:41.290
tray preference and local diagnostics.
Field exposes debugging readiness,
foreground notifications, and

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:42.220
remembered access.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:47.202
You can clear remembered access before
handing the phone back. To finish,

00:06:47.202 --> 00:06:51.374
use Sign out. The product should close any
active session and clear

00:06:51.374 --> 00:06:53.460
the active credentials when you leave.

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:59.007
That completes the Setu walkthrough. The
repository adds a transcript and chapter

00:06:59.007 --> 00:07:03.685
times. You can replay the journeys in the
prototypes using their demo

00:07:03.685 --> 00:07:04.464
scenario menus.
